<main id="content"><header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Umicom Framework</p><h1>Project files and indexed pages</h1><p class="lead">Create project entries and browse a consistent index through shared C contracts.</p></header><p>A file-creation form needs to do more than join two strings. It must identify the workspace, check that the user permits changes there, reject an existing destination and report what actually happened. Framework keeps those rules in one place so different applications can use the same operation.</p>
<h2>Find the implementation</h2><p>The public CMake target is <code>Umicom::platform</code>. Studio's thin facade is <code>UmiStudioUiCreateProjectEntry</code> in <code>applications/studio/src/app/ui.c</code>; it connects the new file to the existing index and document coordinator.</p><div class="table"><table><tr><th>Responsibility</th><th>Source</th></tr><tr><td>Creation contract</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/include/umicom/platform/workspace_files.h"><code>include/umicom/platform/workspace_files.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Platform implementation</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/src/platform/workspace_files.c"><code>src/platform/workspace_files.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Workspace identity and trust</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/include/umicom/platform/workspace_graph.h"><code>include/umicom/platform/workspace_graph.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Index contract</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/include/umicom/platform/file_index.h"><code>include/umicom/platform/file_index.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Index implementation</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/src/platform/file_index.c"><code>src/platform/file_index.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Regression tests</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/tests/test_project_files.c"><code>tests/test_project_files.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Executable example</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/examples/project_files/main.c"><code>examples/project_files/main.c</code></a></td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Separate creation from editing</h2><p><code>UmiWorkspaceCreateEntry</code> creates one empty file or directory. It does not compile the project, open an editor, change a CMake target, or commit to Git. A caller can therefore use the same operation in a visual IDE or another project tool without inheriting Studio's interface.</p>
<p>Supply an open trusted <code>UmiWorkspaceGraph</code>, an expected revision, a relative path, the entry kind and a result structure. A nonzero expected revision protects a form prepared for an earlier workspace state. Zero deliberately accepts the current state. Keep graph mutations and this operation on the graph's owning thread.</p>
<h2>Read the result before continuing</h2><p>A normal success sets <code>result.created</code> and supplies the resolved path. <code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code> means the path was not replaced. An unsuccessful close after exclusive file creation can return an I/O error with <code>created</code> still set. Do not repeat a write or delete the entry simply because the last step failed.</p>
<p>Studio additionally reports <code>index_status</code> and <code>document_status</code>. Successful creation is not rolled back when either subsequent adoption fails: the developer's file remains available. A caller should report these stages separately rather than claiming a file was never created.</p>
<h2>Read a coherent page of indexed files</h2><p><code>UmiFileIndexReadPage</code> copies matching rows and their counts under one index lock. The offset is a position in the filtered results, not a filesystem position. The returned <code>matched</code> is the complete match count, <code>count</code> is the number copied, and <code>has_more</code> says whether another page exists.</p>
<p>Pass a previously observed nonzero revision to reject a page from a changed index. On <code>BUSY</code>, reread the current snapshot and decide whether to return to page one. Output structures are not modified on that failure. A zero-capacity call can obtain counts without copying rows.</p>
<p>A rebuild prepares a private candidate and publishes it only after a successful scan. A concurrent update can make the candidate stale; that rebuild returns <code>BUSY</code> rather than replacing newer index state. No-change refreshes increment the rebuild counter but not the content revision. Revisions describe the published rows and root, not the number of times a refresh button was pressed.</p>
<h2>Memory and lifetime</h2><p>The graph and index are opaque objects owned by their creator. The creation function borrows the graph; the page function copies rows into caller-owned storage and retains no buffer or query pointers. Keep the index alive until all concurrent readers, watchers and rebuilds finish.</p>
<p>Index row storage grows on demand up to the configured file bound. A rebuild temporarily holds the previous and candidate arrays, and several simultaneous scans can each allocate a candidate. This improves small-workspace allocation but is not a process-wide memory budget. The copied rows are metadata, not file contents.</p>
<h2>Build and run a complete client</h2><p>The example below creates a new practice folder, opens an explicit trusted workspace, creates <code>include/notes.h</code>, checks that repeated creation is rejected and reads the header through the page API. It retains the practice project in ordinary use and refuses to overwrite a previous one.</p>
<pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_PROJECT_FILES_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<h3>Windows PowerShell</h3><p>Set the SDK prefix to an installation you have already built and tested; it does not need to have this particular directory name.</p><pre><code>Set-Location &quot;C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications&quot;
$env:Path = &quot;C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path&quot;
cmake -S framework/examples/project_files -B build/notes-project-files -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=&quot;C:/umicom/installed/development&quot; -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=&quot;C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/gcc.exe&quot; -DCMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM=&quot;C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/ninja.exe&quot;
cmake --build build/notes-project-files
ctest --test-dir build/notes-project-files --no-tests=error --output-on-failure
&amp; &quot;.\build\notes-project-files\umicom-notes-project-files.exe&quot;</code></pre>
<h3>Linux shell</h3><pre><code>cd &quot;$HOME/umicom/Umicom-Applications&quot;
cmake -S framework/examples/project_files -B build/notes-project-files -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=&quot;$HOME/umicom/installed/development&quot;
cmake --build build/notes-project-files
ctest --test-dir build/notes-project-files --no-tests=error --output-on-failure
./build/notes-project-files/umicom-notes-project-files</code></pre>
<p>The output includes <code>Indexed: include/notes.h (0 bytes)</code>. That zero is expected: this program creates an empty header. Use Studio's document workflow to add its source text. Run the program from another empty directory for a second independent exercise.</p>
<h2>Path policy and platform boundaries</h2><p>Relative names cannot contain parent traversal, empty components, Git metadata directories, trailing spaces or dots, Windows device aliases or invalid portable filename characters. Parent directories must already exist. Normalising a path is not enough to enforce the rule: the platform implementation also checks the actual parents.</p>
<p>On POSIX systems, creation walks directory descriptors with no-follow checks and uses exclusive creation. The Windows implementation checks directory handles for reparse points, retains the opened parents and uses <code>CREATE_NEW</code>. These checks complement filesystem permissions; they do not establish a general hostile-process sandbox or support every network-filesystem behaviour. Root aliases and platform path-length policies need their own deployment review.</p>
<p>Platform tests should cover exclusive creation, competing creators, inaccessible parents and symlink/reparse fixtures. The provided reparse tests are not executed on Windows by the POSIX fixture; a skipped case is an outstanding check, not a passing result.</p>
<h2>Further reference</h2><p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-createfilew">Microsoft: CreateFileW and creation modes</a> · <a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gobject/method.WeakRef.get.html">GLib: obtaining an owned reference from a weak reference</a></p></main><footer><strong>Umicom Foundation</strong><p>Learn from the source. Build with shared components.</p><a href="../contributing.html">Improve these guides</a> · <a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation">GitHub organisation</a></footer></body></html>
